  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through six innings until the offense broke through late.",
    "what_this_means": "Warren bought them clean innings. Five and two-thirds with no runs allowed. While the lineup stayed patient against Minnesota. The game was locked at 2-2 heading into the eighth, the kind of tight game where one swing matters. Caballero had already delivered the power earlier, two runs driven in on his homer. Rice then broke the tie with a single in the eighth, and what followed was the opposite of stalling: six runs in one inning, the kind of outburst that turns a close game into a statement. This is a team that converts runners on base into actual runs when the moment arrives. The bullpen held what mattered. Fulmer, Hill, and Schreiber combining for a clean finish after Warren's start. Winning 8-3 looks like a blowout on the scoreboard, but it felt like a game decided on one swing at the exact moment it needed to be.",
    "what_to_watch": "Max Fried takes the ball tonight against Bailey Ober, who's carrying a 4.28 ERA into a matchup where the Yankees' recent offensive rhythm. Three straight wins, now into their second in a row. Becomes the question mark. Ober doesn't have the margin for error against a lineup that just proved it can break open a tied game in one inning.",
